WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.392
Thermal imaging senses infrared radiation emitted and reflected by surfaces and converts differences into a visible display.

00:00:08.392 --> 00:00:18.146
The image represents apparent temperature patterns, not ordinary color, material identity, or a complete explanation of what caused the pattern.

00:00:18.146 --> 00:00:24.270
A visible-light camera records reflected light that human eyes can normally see.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:36.745
A thermal sensor works in an infrared range and can reveal contrast that remains invisible to an ordinary camera, especially when surfaces have different thermal behavior.

00:00:36.745 --> 00:00:48.313
Common uses include drone and ground inspections, facility maintenance, electrical or mechanical screening, solar arrays, building-envelope review, search support, fireground awareness, safety, and security.

00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:53.530
Each use requires different equipment, timing, distance, training, and interpretation.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:01:00.562
Thermal imagery can show that one area appears warmer or cooler than its background.

00:01:00.562 --> 00:01:03.510
It usually cannot prove why.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:11.449
Sun exposure, airflow, moisture, material, reflection, geometry, retained heat, and sensor settings may create similar patterns.

00:01:11.449 --> 00:01:16.666
Thermal cameras generally observe radiation from surfaces in their view.

00:01:16.666 --> 00:01:23.697
They do not provide magical vision through walls, roofs, clothing, smoke, or every obstruction.

00:01:23.697 --> 00:01:28.914
Apparent patterns may reflect surface effects rather than hidden conditions.

00:01:28.914 --> 00:01:32.317
Safety and lawful purpose come first.

00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:37.987
Collection must follow aviation, site, electrical, fire, privacy, and organizational controls.

00:01:37.987 --> 00:01:46.380
A thermal sensor does not authorize entry, close approach, intrusive observation, or work beyond the operator's competence.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.689
Treat thermal imagery as evidence that needs context.

00:01:50.689 --> 00:02:01.350
Preserve source files and conditions, compare with expected behavior, seek corroboration, state limitations, and involve a qualified specialist before making high-consequence conclusions.
